---
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ruleJson from '../data/rule.json';
import frontJson from '../data/frontier.json';
import metaJson from '../data/meta.json';
import nullsJson from '../data/nulls.json';
import n3Json from '../data/n3.json';
import dlJson from '../data/downloads.json';

const rule: any = ruleJson, front: any = frontJson, meta: any = metaJson, nulls: any = nullsJson, n3: any = n3Json, dl: any = dlJson;
const sel = rule.selection, ver = rule.verification, vs = rule.verification_status;
const f1 = front.frontier.ec_T1;
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const pct = (x: number, d = 1) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(d)}%`;
const base = url('/');
const kb = (b: number) => (b >= 1e6 ? `${(b / 1e6).toFixed(1)} MB` : `${Math.max(1, Math.round(b / 1000))} KB`);
const order = ['provenance', 'outcomes', 'manifests', 'pool', 'events', 'search', 'verification', 'reports'];
const closed = new Set(['events', 'manifests']);
const freezeShort = meta.hashes.freeze_sha256.slice(0, 16);
const nullB = n3.targets.T1.null.B;
const noise = pct(meta.headline.p_perfect_rule_under_noise.T1, 1);
---
<Base title="Limitations and downloads" description="Everything that could be wrong with this, in one place." current="limits">
  <h1>Limitations and downloads</h1>
  <p class="lede">Everything that could be wrong with this, in one place.</p>

  <h2 id="choice">How this rule was chosen</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The search found {fmt(f1.n_rules)} perfect rules for &ldquo;the incumbent party wins&rdquo;. {sel.tied_top_key_rules} of them tie at the top of the frozen ranking. The featured rule is
      one of those {sel.tied_top_key_rules}, and one of the {sel.tied_sharing_best_cushion} that share the largest minimum timing cushion ({sel.featured_cushion_hours} hours).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>{sel.chosen_by} picked it after seeing the results,</strong> for two reasons: it has no Moon condition, and that cushion. {sel.tied_with_no_moon_literal} of the
      {sel.tied_top_key_rules} tied rules have no Moon condition. Choosing one rule among many equally perfect ones, after the fact, is another way a search like this flatters itself.
    </li>
    <li>Ranks 1&ndash;{sel.tied_top_key_rules} tie on every ranking criterion. Their order is an alphabetical tie-break on condition names, so the rank numbers carry no meaning.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="designed">The search was built to succeed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The sky family (ten bodies, 60 dates, thresholds in steps of 20&deg;) was added on purpose, and sized from tests on shuffled histories so that a perfect rule would be very likely. The identical search on shuffled histories finds one {noise} of the time for T1.</li>
    <li>The choice of facts, dates, thresholds, tiers and cutoffs are all choices. Each was fixed before the real search ran, but they were not chosen blind: several were chosen by looking at how the search behaved on shuffled histories. <a href={url('/how/#decided')}>What was decided when</a> lists them.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>&ldquo;Win&rdquo; was set to the Electoral College before the search.</strong> Under popular-vote coding two elections flip (2000 and 2016), the incumbent-party record goes from 18 wins and 14 losses to 20 and 12, and the perfect rules change completely:
      no rule is perfect under both codings, and the featured rule gets {rule.popvote.correct} of 32 under popular-vote coding (wrong in {rule.popvote.wrong_years.join(' and ')}). The popular-vote version was run once as a side check and <strong>has no shuffled-history comparison</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="crosschecks">What was cross-checked</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Election outcomes:</strong> the National Archives (Electoral College) and UCSB (popular vote) pages agree on the winning party in all 33 elections from 1896, and a third source (Wikipedia lists) agreed with the coded outcomes for all 32 elections in 1900&ndash;2024.</li>
    <li><strong>Sky conditions:</strong> computed with Skyfield and JPL DE421, then compared with JPL Horizons for the featured rule&rsquo;s {ver.horizons_comparisons} values. The truth value differed in {ver.horizons_truth_differs}. That is a second computation from the same family of ephemerides, not an independent observation.</li>
    <li><strong>Super Bowl winners:</strong> Wikipedia checked against ESPN. Nothing else in the featured rule had a second source before the human check, and the human check used ESPN too.</li>
    <li><strong>Everything else in the pool</strong> (most of the sports facts) was built from one source per event family, as listed under Data and sources above, without a second source. The featured rule does not use most of those facts, but many of the other {fmt(f1.n_rules - 1)} perfect rules do.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="data">Data and sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The re-grading of early Stanley Cup facts rests on one source.</strong> Whether the winner of the Cup in 1900, 1904, 1908 and 1912 depends on which challenge series counts was settled by reading
      Wikipedia&rsquo;s list of challenge games, where every series in each year was won by the same club. No independent source was reachable: Hockey-Reference has no pre-1918 pages, and the Britannica and
      hockeydb pages returned errors. The three Wikipedia pages used are not independent of each other. The change took the quarantined literals from 10 to 7 (see the
      <a href={`${base}downloads/reports/phase1_pool_report.md`}>pool report</a>). None of these conditions is in the featured rule.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Other single or thin sources.</strong> Kentucky Derby, Masters and Wimbledon dates come from Wikipedia infoboxes only, and the Wimbledon date is the tournament&rsquo;s end date, standing in for the final.
      NBA game dates are Wikipedia only. The Super Bowl cross-check is ESPN only (two other sites refused access). Club lineage for MLB is read from Wikipedia&rsquo;s labels, not an MLB page. Belmont Stakes run dates
      could not be sourced for 20 of 31 held years, so only the computed &ldquo;won the Triple Crown&rdquo; fact was built. Weather is a single NOAA source, and only New York&rsquo;s Central Park station is complete for every window tested.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Defunct entities.</strong> The criteria say a fact must not depend on a club or country that no longer exists. That was not enforced as a gate. {f1.defunct_rules.count} of the {fmt(f1.n_rules)} perfect rules contain such a fact (the Quebec Bulldogs); they are
      flagged and still counted. The top-ranked rule in the look-ahead test for T1 contains one too (the Seattle Metropolitans). The featured rule contains none. Familiarity grades were set per event, not per club, so an obscure
      defunct club inherits the grade of the Stanley Cup final.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The Venus gap.</strong> In a six-point pilot of JPL Horizons against our astronomy code, five points agreed to about 0.00001&deg;. Venus on 1940-03-06 differed by 0.00027&deg; (about one arcsecond, or 20 seconds of Venus&rsquo;s motion). We did not explain it. Horizons and our code differ in several conventions (ephemeris version, light bending, pre-1962 time handling), and the difference is far smaller than any condition&rsquo;s margin. For the featured rule&rsquo;s own {ver.horizons_comparisons} comparisons, the largest gap was {ver.horizons_max_gap_deg.toFixed(5)}&deg; against a tolerance of {ver.horizons_tolerance_deg}&deg;, and the truth value differed in {ver.horizons_truth_differs} of them.</li>
    <li><strong>Wording.</strong> The sky conditions compare the difference in ecliptic longitude between two bodies at 00:00 UTC, ignoring latitude. The pool&rsquo;s own text called this &ldquo;angular separation&rdquo;; only the display text was corrected, and the data files keep the original wording.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weather had coverage failures.</strong> Station records have gaps and moves, and stations were never spliced. For the summer-temperature and first-snow windows tested, only New York&rsquo;s Central Park station has complete data for all 32 election years.
      Chicago, Washington, Boston and Los Angeles stations each miss years, so those cities were left out rather than patched. Readings flagged as bad by NOAA count as missing.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Timezones.</strong> Some sky conditions would flip if the instant were local midnight in the US; they are flagged as timezone-sensitive. None is in the featured rule.</li>
    <li>Olympic closing dates differ between sources for 1904, 1908, 1912 and 1920; no before-Election-Day truth value depends on the difference. Pre-1936 college football and the Heisman Trophy were not used.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="stats">Statistical caveats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The look-ahead comparison used {fmt(nullB)} shuffled histories per target,</strong> not the 20,000 planned, because finding the top-ranked rule exactly is slow when thousands of rules fit the 21 training elections (the run took more than
      three hours). With {fmt(nullB)} shuffles a share near 50% is known to within about &plusmn;2 percentage points. <strong>The second split from the project plan (fit 1900&ndash;1990, score 1992&ndash;2024) was not run.</strong>
    </li>
    <li>The party-level shuffle set aside {pct(nulls.party.rejected_fraction, 1)} of its draws because the derived incumbent-party target had too few true years for exact counting. The structured shuffles of the real sequence are only 63 exact sequences plus block shuffles.</li>
    <li>The shuffles keep the number of wins and shuffle everything else. They test whether the search can find a perfect rule in a history with no information in it. They do not test any particular mechanism.</li>
    <li>&ldquo;Perfect&rdquo; here means all 32 elections right, using at most four facts. Counts of perfect rules are counts of minimal combinations of distinct patterns; many facts share the same pattern across years.</li>
    <li>A single split, 1900&ndash;1980 against 1984&ndash;2024, was used for the look-ahead test, and it was fixed in advance.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="process">How the work was done</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The runner that produced the real-search outputs is not part of the frozen set.</strong> The frozen driver could only write one file with the top 40 rules and no log, so a small orchestrator (<code>src/search/gate2.py</code>) called the same frozen
      search functions with an unlimited rule list and wrote to a separate folder. It was committed before the run and tested only on shuffled noise. The look-ahead test&rsquo;s shortcut for finding the top-ranked rule is also unfrozen; it was checked against full
      enumeration on 40 random histories. The pools, cutoffs, ranking and procedure were not changed.
    </li>
    <li>The real search was run once. Nothing was rerun, re-ranked or tuned afterward. The pipeline hash <code title={meta.hashes.freeze_sha256}>{freezeShort}&hellip;</code> covers the config, pools, astronomy grid, search code, cutoffs and seeds.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Human checking is thin: {vs.human_checked} of {vs.rows} rows human-checked, {vs.automated_only} automated.</strong>
      {ver.human_checks.length > 0 && <> The human-checked rows are the Pittsburgh Steelers&rsquo; Super Bowl wins in {ver.human_checks.map((h: any) => h.year).join(' and ')} (checked by {ver.human_checks[0].checked_by} against <a href={ver.human_checks[0].source_url}>ESPN</a>, {ver.human_checks[0].checked_on}).</>}
      That is the same source the automated Super Bowl cross-check used, so it adds a human reader, not an independent source.
      The automated checks (JPL Horizons for the sky conditions, ESPN for the Super Bowl, a second source for election outcomes) are real but are not a person reading the sources.
    </li>
    <li>The site copy is marked draft until approved.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="claim">What a real claim would need</h2>
  <p>
    One rule, written down before the outcome is known, from a pool fixed beforehand, with no choosing among tied candidates afterward, tested prospectively on an election that has not happened. The next one is in 2028, and <a href={url('/2028/')}>the 2028 page</a> shows what this rule and its equally perfect rivals say for it.
    Nothing here meets that bar, and nothing here was meant to.
  </p>

  <h2 id="downloads">Downloads</h2>
  <p>
    These are derived files and source <em>manifests</em>. The source web pages themselves are not redistributed: each manifest lists the page&rsquo;s URL, retrieval date and a sha256 so the page can be fetched again and checked.
    The manifest for the National Archives and UCSB pages used for the election outcomes lists file names and sha256 only; the URL patterns are in the accompanying sources note. Per-shuffle files from the
    null runs are omitted for size; they regenerate from the seeds. {fmt(dl.files.length)} files, {kb(dl.total_bytes)} in total.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Retrieval dates.</strong> The combined source manifest (<a href={`${base}downloads/manifests/ALL_SOURCES_MANIFEST.csv`}>ALL_SOURCES_MANIFEST.csv</a>) lists URL, retrieval date and sha256 for every source page.
    For the National Archives and UCSB pages used for election outcomes, the original manifest recorded only file and sha256: their URLs are rebuilt from the published URL patterns, and the date is the one batch date
    (2026-10-25) written in the sources note, not a per-file record. The <code>url_basis</code> column says which rows are which. The derived files below are built from the repository, not downloaded, so they have a sha256 and no URL.
  </p>
  {order.map((g) => {
    const files = dl.files.filter((f: any) => f.group === g);
    return (
      <details class="viz-table" open={!closed.has(g)}>
        <summary>{dl.groups[g]} ({files.length})</summary>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it is</th><th class="r">Size</th><th>sha256</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {files.map((f: any) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><a href={`${base}${f.path}`} download>{f.name}</a></td>
                  <td>{f.description}</td>
                  <td class="r">{kb(f.bytes)}</td>
                  <td><code title={f.sha256}>{f.sha256.slice(0, 12)}&hellip;</code></td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>
    );
  })}
  <h3>Reproducing the numbers</h3>
  <p class="sub">From the project repository (not yet public):</p>
  <pre class="repro"><code>uv sync
uv run pytest                                  # 50+ tests, including the frozen-state checks
uv run python -c "import yaml; from src.nulls.freeze import build; fz = yaml.safe_load(open('freeze.yaml')); print(build(fz['null_plan'])['freeze_sha256'] == fz['freeze_sha256'])"   # True if the frozen files are unchanged
uv run python -m src.site.export               # rebuild the data behind these pages (refuses if hashes differ)
cd site && npm install && npx vitest run       # TypeScript search port against Python results</code></pre>
</Base>
